# tunnel-puck settings for one puck: the puck itself. Make yours with
# scripts/tpk-ctl.sh new (or copy this file to ~/wg-tunnels/pucks/<name>/puck.conf)
# and fill it in. The name is the puck's: up to 10 of a-z, 0-9 and -, starting
# with a letter, e.g. pucks/home/puck.conf. Shell syntax: NAME="value".
# scripts/tpk-ctl.sh reads it on your computer: tpk-ctl.sh <name> <command> (the name
# can be left out while there's only one). No secrets go in here: the Cloudflare
# token stays in its own file.
#
# Each gateway the puck serves (the WireGuard server your clients connect to) has
# its own file, pucks/<name>/gateways/<gateway>.conf (puck.gateway.conf.example):
# tpk-ctl.sh <name> gateway add <gateway> makes it. The Wi-Fi list, if any, is
# pucks/<name>/wifi.conf (puck.wifi.conf.example).

# tpk-ctl.sh reaches the puck the first way that answers, and says which it used:
#   1. SSH_LAN, on your home network while you build it
#   2. through each gateway over its WireGuard link, the everyday way after drop-off
#   3. SSH_CLOUDFLARE, the emergency door (below), when the links are down
SSH_LAN="pi@tunnel-puck.local"      # user@name of the puck on your home network (the user and hostname set when flashing it)

# Optional: the emergency door. SSH through a Cloudflare Tunnel, which doesn't
# depend on the WireGuard links, so you can still get in and fix the puck when
# they're down (a blocked port, a changed gateway IP, a bad change). Costs a domain
# on Cloudflare and step 1.2b. Empty: no Cloudflare; if the links are down after
# drop-off, the puck must be power-cycled, or brought home.
SSH_CLOUDFLARE=""                   # e.g. ssh.puck.example.com, a hostname on your Cloudflare domain
CF_TOKEN_FILE="$HOME/.config/tunnel-puck/cloudflared.token"    # with SSH_CLOUDFLARE: the tunnel token alone, mode 600
